<div class="q-page">
  <QuorumWeb.Shell.header current_user={@conn.assigns[:current_user]} />

  <main class="q-prose">
    <p class="q-draft">
      This is a working draft.
    </p>

    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="q-prose-lead">
      Students don't have accounts and aren't asked for anything that identifies them. Lecturers
      give an email address and nothing else.
    </p>

    <h2>If you're a student</h2>
    <p>
      Joining a lecture needs no name, no email, and no account. Your browser is given one random
      token, kept in a cookie, and that token does two things: it stops the same browser voting
      twice on a question, and it lets you retract a question you posted. It isn't linked to you,
      and it tells us nothing about who you are.
    </p>
    <p>
      What's stored is the text of the questions you post, the votes you cast, and a display name
      only if you choose to type one. Your lecturer sees the questions and the vote counts. They
      don't see which browser cast which vote.
    </p>

    <h2>If you're a lecturer</h2>
    <p>
      Signing in stores your email address and the single-use links sent to it. Those links expire
      after {Quorum.Accounts.link_ttl_minutes()} minutes and are spent the first time they're used.
      Rooms you open while signed in are linked to your account so you can find them again.
    </p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
      One cookie, <code>_quorum_key</code>, holding the session. It carries the random browser
      token described above, and your user id once you sign in. There are no advertising cookies
      and no analytics cookies.
    </p>

    <h2>Third parties</h2>
    <p>
      There are none in the page. No analytics, no tag managers, no embedded trackers. Typefaces
      are served from Quorum's own servers rather than a font CDN, so opening a page makes no
      request to anyone else.
    </p>

    <h2>Email</h2>
    <p>
      Quorum sends two kinds of message: a sign-in link to a lecturer who asked for one, and the
      contact form's message to us. Nothing else, and no marketing.
    </p>

    <h2>How long questions are kept</h2>
    <p>
      Questions and their votes outlive the session by default. A term of them is what tells a
      lecturer which material didn't land, so keeping them is what the room is for rather than a
      default nobody chose. A lecturer can turn that off per room, and closing the session then
      deletes that room's questions and votes outright.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in a kept question identifies the student who posted it. What's stored is the text,
      the vote counts, a display name only if one was typed, and the random browser token
      described above.
    </p>

    <h2>Still to decide</h2>
    <p>
      How long rooms themselves are kept, the named data controller, and the governing
      jurisdiction. These belong in the published version and are open questions today rather than
      settled ones.
    </p>

    <p class="q-prose-cta">
      Questions about any of this? <.link navigate={~p"/contact"} class="q-button--link">Contact us</.link>.
    </p>
  </main>

  <QuorumWeb.Shell.footer />
</div>
